      "number": 1,
      "title": "Your head is older than your feet",
      "status": "rendering",
      "youtube": {
        "title": "Your head is older than your feet",
        "description": "Your head ticks a tiny bit faster than your feet. Gravity slows time, so the higher you are, the faster your time runs.\n\nIn 2010, physicists at NIST, a U.S. lab, raised an atomic clock by about a foot (two steps up a staircase). The higher clock ticked faster. Over a 79-year life, that adds up to about 90 billionths of a second. GPS satellite clocks run fast too, so engineers set them slower before launch.\n\nRule No. 1: the higher you are, the faster your time runs.\n\nSource: NIST news release, 23 Sept 2010.\n\n#Shorts",
        "hashtags": [
          "#Shorts",
          "#Science",
          "#Physics",
          "#Relativity",
          "#UnrealActually"
        ]
      },
      "tiktok": {
        "caption": "Your head is older than your feet. Gravity slows time, so the higher you are, the faster your time runs. NIST tested it with two atomic clocks, one raised about a foot. The higher one ticked faster.",
        "hashtags": [
          "#science",
          "#physics",
          "#relativity",
          "#learnontiktok",
          "#UnrealActually"
        ]
      },
      "instagram": {
        "caption": "Unreal, Actually · Ep 1\n\nYour head is older than your feet. Gravity slows time, so the higher you are, the faster your time runs. In 2010, NIST raised an atomic clock by about a foot, and it ticked faster. Over a lifetime, about 90 billionths of a second. Far too small to feel, but real.",
        "hashtags": [
          "#science",
          "#physics",
